  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species is known from Senegal to Ethiopia, and along the entire length of the Nile.<br/><br/><strong>Eastern Africa:</strong> It is present in the upper Nile system, and Lake Turkana.<br/><br/><strong>Northern Africa:</strong> This species is known from upper Egyptian Nile.<br/><br/><strong>Northeast Africa:</strong> This species is found in Lake Nasser (also known as Lake Nubia), Sudan, as well as the Baro River, Ethiopia.<br/><br/><strong>Western Africa:</strong> This species is known from the Chad basin, Niger, Volta, Gambia, and Senegal Rivers.",
    "population": "No information is available on the population.",
    "habitats": "This species occurs in large rivers, open waters, macrophyte beds and vegetated fringes where it feeds on molluscs (Froese and Pauly 2003). It rises to the surface to inflate its body resembling a balloon, which gives it protection from predators by making it harder to eat (Lévêque and Paugy 2003).",
    "threats": "Climate change and the reduction of water flow threaten this species across its range. In northern Africa, water pollution from agricultural, domestic and commercial/industrial sources poses a possible threat. In western Africa, fishing activities could lead to a possible reduction in the population in the future.",
    "measures": "No conservation actions are known to be in place for this species. More research is needed into the population size and range, biology and ecology, harvest, habitat status and threats, as well as monitoring and potential conservation measures.",
    "use_trade": "It is unmarketable for food. Some authors reported that its flesh may be poisonous due to the presence of tetradontoxine (Bishai and Khalil 1997). There are unknown levels of captive breeding for the aquarium trade.",
    "rationale": "This species has a wide distribution. Although there are threats acting across the range, these are not thought to be causing declines significant enough to place the species in a threatened category or as Near Threatened. Therefore, it is listed as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "Cited by Bishai and Khalil (1997) as <em>Tetrodon linneatus</em> Linnaeus, 1766"
  },
